  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nSurface high pressure centered over the Great Lakes is providing \nlovely weather today. Midday temperatures are hovering in the low to \nmid 80s. Dew points have mixed down into the mid 50s across parts of \nsouthern IL and generally low 60s elsewhere. Likely will see our \nstring of consecutive days hitting 90 degrees end at 9 days except \nperhaps our far south/southwest.\n\nUpper level ridging currently over the Southern Plains will build \nback into our region over the next couple of days. Southerly flow \nwill usher in a return of higher humidity as dew points nudge up \ninto the upper 60s to low 70s again. Heat index readings should \ngenerally stay below 100 in most locations though. Wednesday looks \nto be the hottest day as high temperatures reach 95 to 100 combined \nwith continued high humidity to produce heat index values of 100 to \n108. HeatRisk is up to level 3 again on Wednesday and it's quite \npossible a Heat Advisory may be needed.\n\nShortwave energy sweeping across the northern tier of the U.S. will \nallow a cold front to push south into our region Wednesday night \ninto Thursday. This will lead to a good shot of rain, something that \nwill certainly be needed, given the dry pattern that has developed. \nQPF looks greatest across our north where there is an 80% chance of \nat least 0.25\" and a 40-50% chance of 0.50\" through Thursday night. \nMeanwhile places along the AR/TN borders have only about a 40% \nchance at receiving at least 0.25\" via the LREF. The overall threat \nfor severe storms is fairly low, but a few are possible. Seems the \nbest chance for those could be Wednesday night along and north of I-\n64 but there could be another window Thursday afternoon across our \nsouth if we manage enough clearing for higher instability to \ndevelop. \n\nWeak troughing persists across the region Friday into Saturday along \nwith lingering low level moisture (dew points remaining in the low \n70). Therefore, it may end up staying rather unsettled with more \ncloud cover and at least small chances for rain continuing. Guidance \nhas been hinting at the ridge building back into our region yet \nagain by Sunday into Monday next week. Most solutions keep it short-\nlived though with lower heights arriving by mid week. LREF has a \ngood likelihood (50-70%) of high temperatures hitting 90 again by \nSunday-Monday.",
